1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,765
You checked an app's code.

2
00:00:02,765 --> 00:00:05,240
Could that code change later?

3
00:00:05,490 --> 00:00:06,553
On Tari Ootle,

4
00:00:06,553 --> 00:00:08,822
every component gets an upgrade

5
00:00:08,822 --> 00:00:10,523
rule when it's created.

6
00:00:10,523 --> 00:00:13,650
And that rule can't be changed afterwards.

7
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:15,531
Locked, the default,

8
00:00:15,531 --> 00:00:18,160
means nobody can upgrade its code.

9
00:00:18,410 --> 00:00:20,802
Owner means the component's owner

10
00:00:20,802 --> 00:00:22,570
can approve an upgrade.

11
00:00:22,820 --> 00:00:24,824
And an access rule hands

12
00:00:24,824 --> 00:00:27,068
that power to someone else,

13
00:00:27,068 --> 00:00:28,500
like a multisig.

14
00:00:28,750 --> 00:00:31,170
So before using an app, ask:

15
00:00:31,170 --> 00:00:33,841
can this component be upgraded,

16
00:00:33,841 --> 00:00:35,910
and who can approve it?

17
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:37,436
One more thing.

18
00:00:37,436 --> 00:00:40,193
Locked code is not a safety certificate.

19
00:00:40,193 --> 00:00:41,336
Bugs can remain,

20
00:00:41,336 --> 00:00:43,622
and owner-controlled settings can

21
00:00:43,622 --> 00:00:45,520
still change its behavior.

22
00:00:45,870 --> 00:00:49,404
The real question isn't just, is it on-chain?

23
00:00:49,404 --> 00:00:49,866
It's:

24
00:00:49,866 --> 00:00:53,550
what can change, and who controls that change?

25
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:54,954
Independent explainer.

26
00:00:54,954 --> 00:00:56,960
Tari's documentation is linked below.
